WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.580
Michael Keaton's Batman officially returns in&nbsp;
"The Flash." We've known about it for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.100
but actually getting to see him on screen again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.660
has set the Internet wild with excitement.&nbsp;
Here's what everybody online had to say.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:17.340
The highly anticipated Super Bowl&nbsp;
LVII trailer for "The Flash" got fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.260
buzzing before the big game on Sunday. The&nbsp;
blockbuster tease presented the anxious DC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:27.540
fandom with several jaw-dropping revelations&nbsp;
and eye-opening little details. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.680
the fans have a fair share of things to be&nbsp;
excited about when the film drops in June.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.360
During the trailer, not one, but two Barry&nbsp;
Allens unite to try and undo the damage done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:41.280
when the Flash breaks the Speed Force, causing&nbsp;
an existential meltdown across timelines.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.180
Newcomer Supergirl and a returning&nbsp;
General Zod have their moments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.560
but it's Michael Keaton's return as Batman&nbsp;
that really put this trailer over the top.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.460
Keaton reprises the dual roles&nbsp;
of billionaire Bruce Wayne and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.134
the Dark Knight detective he originally&nbsp;
portrayed in Tim Burton's 1989 "Batman."

00:01:01.081 --> 00:01:02.376
"Yeah. I'm Batman."

00:01:02.376 --> 00:01:07.500
For Keaton, it's been nearly 35 years since&nbsp;
he first suited up to fight crime in Gotham&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.700
City. But returning to the role and putting&nbsp;
the Batsuit back on after all these years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.260
was easier than fans might think. In an&nbsp;
interview with "Jake's Takes" Keaton said,

00:01:16.416 --> 00:01:21.816
"It was shockingly normal — weird. And&nbsp;
like I went, 'Oh, oh yeah, that's right.'"

00:01:21.816 --> 00:01:27.120
The return of Keaton's Batman is arguably the&nbsp;
high-water mark for "The Flash" despite all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.980
its cool characters, even the film's titular&nbsp;
star. And it seems the fans will accept almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.640
anything "The Flash" has to offer thanks&nbsp;
to Keaton's involvement in the project.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.801
One YouTuber posted in the&nbsp;
comments of the new trailer:

00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:42.180
"Seeing Michael Keaton as the&nbsp;
Dark Knight again after 34 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:46.020
just brought a tear to my eye.&nbsp;
This movie is going to be EPIC."

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.960
Keaton's crime-fighting Bruce Wayne last appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.220
in 1992's "Batman Returns," which&nbsp;
was also helmed by Tim Burton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:58.200
and there's no denying that the DC fandom is&nbsp;
happy to have him back. Another commenter wrote:

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.309
"Not gonna lie. I got teary-eyed&nbsp;
seeing Michael Keaton back as Batman."

00:02:02.309 --> 00:02:04.920
Keaton's version of the Dark&nbsp;
Knight seems like he's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.520
to be a major player in "The Flash,"&nbsp;
judging from the Super Bowl trailer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:12.660
and many of the fans are incredibly&nbsp;
grateful. Another YouTuber wrote,

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:18.278
"Words can't express how much it means to me to&nbsp;
see Michael Keaton back as Batman. Thank you."

00:02:18.278 --> 00:02:22.560
Most of the chatter following "The Flash"&nbsp;
Super Bowl drop seems to be focused on the Bat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.860
so it shouldn't come as a surprise&nbsp;
that fans are willing to put up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.434
multiple Ezra Millers just to&nbsp;
see Keaton don the cowl again.

00:02:30.423 --> 00:02:33.140
"It's my face. You stole my face."

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.740
One user on Twitter posted,

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:39.780
"I'm up for DC disappointing me again just to&nbsp;
watch Michael Keaton play Batman one more time."

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.640
And Miller may be playing the titular role, but&nbsp;
some fans will buy tickets for the sole purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:49.440
of witnessing Keaton clad in the Batsuit&nbsp;
again. Composer Brian Ralston tweeted,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.440
"I hope Warner Bros. Pictures&nbsp;
properly interprets that people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:56.460
are going to see this film (and it will&nbsp;
be successful) because of Michael Keaton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.420
returning to be Batman in it and not&nbsp;
because of Ezra Miller. Just saying."

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:04.020
For other fans, Miller's off-screen issues&nbsp;
might have kept them from watching "The Flash"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.940
altogether had it not been for the inclusion&nbsp;
of Keaton's Batman. One Twitter user said,

00:03:09.162 --> 00:03:12.882
"I was mad when they didn't pull this&nbsp;
movie because of all the actor was doing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.882 --> 00:03:17.691
but I really want to see this. Especially&nbsp;
Michael Keaton, he's the best."

00:03:17.691 --> 00:03:22.080
Hopefully, the hype surrounding Keaton's&nbsp;
return and the film will pay off come June.

